WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:07.220
ROGER MARTINEZ:
OK, so today, I'm

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:09.470
going to talk about Google
Cloud's managed instance

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:13.225
groups, specifically the
auto healing feature.

00:00:13.225 --> 00:00:14.850
So, to show you what
I'm talking about,

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:19.060
let's head over here to the
navigation menu on the left.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.100
Then down to Compute Engine.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.160
And then over to
Instance Groups.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.100
So what I have on this
project is one managed

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.780
and since group called WebApp.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:29.300
There it is.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.190
Let's click the name to
go to the Overview page.

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:33.940
And you'll see that
this instance group is

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.160
comprised of eight instances.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:41.260
So each of these instances
is just an HTTP application.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.750
And it spits out some
basic instance information,

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.610
and also has some
built-in functionality

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:48.430
that lets me change
its health state.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:50.480
Let's go back to
the Overview page.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.450
So as you can see,
each of these instances

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.850
is currently reporting
back as healthy.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:57.520
And the reason for that is that
I have auto healing configured

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.390
on this managed instance group.

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.460
So let's check out
that configuration.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.920
So when you create a new
managed interest group,

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.390
or when you edit
an existing one,

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:10.030
you'll find the auto healing
configuration down here

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.510
at the bottom.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:16.030
And all it really is
is a health check.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:18.030
So any health check that
you have on the project

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:23.980
will show up right here, or you
can create a new one up here.

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.500
It's worth noting that these
health checks are the same ones

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.600
that you would use to
configure a load balancer.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.767
So if you want to use a
load balancer in conjunction

00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:32.470
with auto healing,
you want to make sure

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:35.200
that the health check that you
use for your load balancer is

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.810
more aggressive, and checks
more frequently than the one you

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:39.160
use here for auto healing.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.080
So the reason for that
is that if something

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.158
goes wrong with one
of your instances,

00:01:43.158 --> 00:01:45.700
you'd want your load balancer
to start directing traffic away

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.860
from that instance
before auto healing gets

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:49.740
started with recreating
that instance.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.490
So once you've selected
your health check,

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:54.430
you need to specify an
appropriate initial delay.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:56.825
So the initial delay tells
the instance group manager

00:01:56.825 --> 00:01:58.450
how long before it
should start running

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.660
the health check against the
instance after its provision.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.300
This is especially important if
you've got any startup scripts

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.190
or configuration
management that needs

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:09.207
to complete before an instance
is ready to do its job.

00:02:09.207 --> 00:02:11.290
So I know that this
particular application doesn't

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:12.415
take very long to start up.

00:02:12.415 --> 00:02:14.875
So I've set my initial
delay to just 15 seconds.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.370
So if you like to
use the CLI, you

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:25.950
can use gcloud compute
instance-groups managed

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.550
list-instances followed by the
name of the managed interest

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.440
group and the region.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.040
And they'll return the
instances that are currently

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.310
in your managed instance group,
along with the zone, status,

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:41.460
and health state.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.955
So let's keep this
running down here while we

00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:45.750
see auto healing in action.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.990
So let's select one of
these instances up here.

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:49.310
1RJ5.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:50.270
Hit the front end.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.790
And let's make it unhealthy.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:54.710
And now that it's
unhealthy, the health check

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.010
should pick up on it.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.010
So to see what's going
on, let's actually

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:00.510
see the health check that I
have configured for this managed

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.890
instance group by going over
here, down to Health Checks.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:05.120
And there's my health check.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.520
HTTP health check
in use by WebApp.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.500
And as you can see down
here, that instance

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.050
is already picked up
as being unhealthy

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.960
and it's currently
being recreated.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.900
So let's check out
this configuration.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.360
Let's hit Edit.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.820
So what I have here is
an HTTP health check

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:27.710
that is checking
against Port 80,

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.840
and requesting the path health.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.230
But you can select from these
five different protocols.

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:34.890
If you scroll down
here to the bottom,

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.450
you'll get the health criteria.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.180
So according to
the configuration,

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.150
this health check will
check each of my instances

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:42.860
every 5 seconds.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:46.040
If it doesn't get a response
it wants within 4 seconds,

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.170
it's going to count
that as a failure.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:50.000
Three consecutive
failures is going

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.293
to mark that instance
as being unhealthy.

00:03:52.293 --> 00:03:53.960
And it's going to
keep that health state

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:57.830
as unhealthy or unknown until it
has two consecutive successes.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.110
So my managed instance
group is in the process

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.360
of recreating 1RJ5.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:06.340
And soon, it'll be back up and
running in a healthy state.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:09.240
So in conclusion, using auto
healing in a managed instance

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.820
group makes sure that your
instances are running the way

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.087
that they're supposed to.

00:04:13.087 --> 00:04:14.670
And in any case that
they aren't, they

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.310
can quickly and automatically
be reprovisioned and made

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:19.920
available to make sure that your
managed instance group is up

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.110
and running at full capacity.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.780
So that was just a quick look at
Google Cloud's manage instance

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:27.150
groups auto healing feature.

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

